Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.37,0:00:02.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Second: the spring, in full.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.43,0:00:08.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}If the data lie in a flat subspace,\Nthe ideal {\c&HF09CB4&}wind{\c&HF7F4F2&} splits in two.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.82,0:00:11.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Along it, a part a network can learn.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.05,0:00:18.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Across it, a pull back: the stray part,\Ndivided by the time left on the clock.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.96,0:00:23.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Followed exactly, that pull lands\Nevery sample in the subspace.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.12,0:00:26.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Trained perfectly, the plain generator,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:26.84,0:00:30.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}both projections and hill\Nmaps give the same crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.46,0:00:35.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Cap the pull, and an\Norder-one stray part survives.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:35.76,0:00:41.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now add a small, bounded error to the\Nexact pull. Does a stray part survive?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:44.83,0:00:47.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. The pull erases it.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:47.61,0:00:50.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the plain generator's\Nzero point four one
Dialogue: 0,0:00:50.71,0:00:54.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}reads as a missing pull,\Nnot a small error.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:54.82,0:00:58.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the projected flow's tiny\Nresidue? Single precision.
